{"command": "cat /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# Feature Specification: Atomic Batch Journal Writer (BatchWriter)\n\n**Feature Branch**: `[001-transactions]`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft (updated for milestone 2 and Claim-A extensions R05b / R05 clarification)\n\n**Input**: User description: \"tinydb milestone 1: Build tinydb.journal.BatchWriter(db), wrapping a supplied TinyDB instance without changing its ordinary APIs. Use the default table only. R01: apply(operations) accepts a list of operation dictionaries. Each operation is either {'op':'insert','document':dict} or {'op':'remove','doc_id':positive integer}. Return a list of inserted document IDs (removes add no result). Empty input returns []. Unknown/malformed operations raise ValueError. R02: Batch application is atomic: validate and apply sequentially, but on any error restore the complete pre-batch default-table data. A remove targeting a missing ID is an error. Previously acquired db.table('_default') handles must reflect both success and rollback. R03: Ordinary TinyDB reads/writes and preexisting documents continue to work. Inserted and returned nested data must not alias the caller's input. Mutating an operation after apply cannot mutate the database.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Apply a batch of insert/remove operations (Priority: P1)\n\nA caller holding a TinyDB instance wraps it with a BatchWriter and submits a\nbatch of operations as a list of operation dictionaries. Each operation either\ninserts a document (possibly with nested lists/dicts) into the default table or\nremoves an existing document by its positive integer document ID. The batch is\nvalidated and applied sequentially, and the caller receives the list of inserted\ndocument IDs in insertion order; remove operations contribute no result.\n\n**Why this priority**: This is the core capability the feature exists to\nprovide. Without it the feature has no value; with it alone the feature is a\nviable, testable MVP.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by creating a TinyDB instance, wrapping\nit with BatchWriter, calling apply() with a mixed insert/remove batch, and\nasserting the returned ID list and the resulting default-table contents.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a default table containing preexisting documents, **When**\n   apply() is called with a non-empty list of valid insert and remove\n   operations, **Then** all operations are applied in order to the default\n   table and a list of the inserted document IDs (insertion order; removes add\n   nothing) is returned.\n2. **Given** a BatchWriter, **When** apply() is called with an empty list,\n   **Then** [] is returned and the default table is unchanged.\n3. **Given** a batch containing an unknown or malformed operation (op not\n   'insert'/'remove', missing or wrong-typed fields, non-dict operation,\n   non-list input, or a doc_id that is not a positive integer), **When**\n   apply() is called, **Then** a ValueError is raised and no partial changes\n   remain in the default table.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Atomic all-or-nothing batch (Priority: P2)\n\nA caller submits a batch that fails partway through (for example, a remove\ntargeting a missing document ID, or a malformed operation after several\nsuccessful ones). The whole batch is rolled back: the default table is restored\nto its complete pre-batch state, and previously acquired handles to the default\ntable observe the rollback. On success, those same handles observe the applied\nchanges.\n\n**Why this priority**: Atomicity is the safety property that makes batch\napplication trustworthy; it is the second pillar of the milestone after basic\napplication.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by seeding the default table, acquiring\na table handle, applying a batch that fails mid-way, and asserting the table\nhandle shows exactly the pre-batch data; then applying a valid batch and\nasserting the handle shows the updated data.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a non-empty pre-batch default table and a batch whose later\n   operation targets a missing document ID, **When** apply() is called,\n   **Then** an error is raised and the default table is restored to its\n   complete pre-batch data (documents inserted earlier in the same batch are\n   removed, documents removed earlier in the same batch are restored).\n2. **Given** a previously acquired handle to the default table, **When** a\n   successful batch is applied, **Then** the handle reflects the applied\n   changes; when a failing batch is applied, **Then** the handle reflects the\n   rolled-back pre-batch data.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Non-invasive wrap with independent data copies (Priority: P3)\n\nA caller uses BatchWriter alongside ordinary TinyDB usage. Wrapping the\ninstance does not change ordinary TinyDB reads or writes, and preexisting\ndocuments remain accessible and mutable through the ordinary API. Data inserted\nvia a batch and data returned by apply() are independent copies: mutating the\ncaller's input documents or the returned IDs' associated data afterwards cannot\nmutate the database contents.\n\n**Why this priority**: Compatibility and data-isolation guarantees protect\nexisting consumers; they refine the feature rather than define its core value.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by inserting documents through the\nordinary API and through a batch, then mutating the caller's original input\ndictionaries and verifying the database contents are unchanged, and by\nperforming ordinary TinyDB reads/writes before and after batch use.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a TinyDB instance with preexisting documents, **When** it is\n   wrapped with BatchWriter and ordinary TinyDB reads/writes are performed\n   before and after batch operations, **Then** all reads/writes behave exactly\n   as without the wrapper.\n2. **Given** a successful batch insert of a nested document, **When** the\n   caller mutates the original input document afterwards, **Then** the stored\n   document is unchanged; the returned inserted IDs identify the stored\n   documents.\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- Empty operations list: returns [] and leaves the table unchanged.\n- Remove targeting a missing document ID: an error; the whole batch rolls back.\n- doc_id that is 0, negative, or not an integer: ValueError (malformed).\n- Operation that is not a dictionary, or a list that is not a list of\n  dictionaries: ValueError.\n- Insert whose 'document' value is not a dict: ValueError.\n- A remove that targets a document inserted earlier in the same batch: allowed\n  (sequential application; the document exists at that point).\n- Failure after several successful operations: complete rollback of the\n  pre-batch state, including undoing earlier inserts and restores of earlier\n  removes within the same batch.\n- Nested documents (dicts and lists at any depth) in inserts: stored and\n  returned as copies, not aliases of the caller's input.\n- doc_id given as a boolean (True/False): ValueError (bool is not accepted\n  as a positive integer).\n- Operation dict carrying fields beyond its op kind (e.g. an insert with a\n  'doc_id' field or a remove with a 'document' field): ValueError.\n- preview() called with a batch that would fail: raises the same error apply\n  would raise, consumes no IDs, and leaves data and the next insertion ID\n  unchanged.\n- preview() called twice (or a returned list mutated): each call returns a\n  fresh list; mutation of an earlier result changes nothing.\n- Direct db.insert between wrapper calls: the next apply/preview continues\n  the ID sequence from the state including that direct insert.\n- Successful batch containing removes: the next insertion ID still advances\n  past every ID the batch consumed (no reuse after removes).\n\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n- **FR-001** (R01): BatchWriter MUST wrap a supplied TinyDB instance and expose\n  apply(operations), which accepts a list of operation dictionaries.\n- **FR-002** (R01): Each operation MUST be either {'op': 'insert',\n  'document': dict} or {'op': 'remove', 'doc_id': positive integer}; no other\n  operation kinds are supported; only the default table is used.\n- **FR-003** (R01): apply() MUST return the list of inserted document IDs in\n  insertion order; remove operations MUST add no result; empty input MUST\n  return [].\n- **FR-004** (R01): Unknown or malformed operations MUST raise ValueError\n  (including: op not 'insert'/'remove', missing or wrong-typed required fields,\n  doc_id not a positive integer, document not a dict, non-dict operation,\n  non-list input).\n- **FR-005** (R02): Batch application MUST be atomic: operations are validated\n  and applied sequentially, but on any error the complete pre-batch default-\n  table data MUST be restored and no partial changes may remain.\n- **FR-006** (R02): A remove targeting a missing document ID MUST be an error\n  and MUST trigger the rollback in FR-005.\n- **FR-007** (R02): Previously acquired handles to the default table (the table\n  obtained via db.table('_default') before the batch) MUST reflect both\n  successful application and rollback.\n- **FR-008** (R03): Ordinary TinyDB reads/writes and preexisting documents MUST\n  continue to work when a BatchWriter wraps the instance; the wrapper MUST NOT\n  change ordinary TinyDB behavior.\n- **FR-009** (R03): Inserted and returned nested data MUST NOT alias the\n  caller's input; mutating an operation (or its document) after apply() MUST\n  NOT mutate the database.\n- **FR-010** (R04): A boolean MUST be rejected as a doc_id even though bool\n  is an int subclass; operations MUST be validated structurally before any\n  change is committed; ValueError MUST be raised for unsupported fields, a\n  non-list operations argument, or a non-dict document; an empty dictionary\n  MUST be accepted as a document.\n- **FR-011** (R05): BatchWriter MUST expose preview(operations), returning the\n  inserted document IDs that apply() would produce while leaving all table\n  data and the next insertion ID unchanged, including when the batch would\n  fail; preview and a failed apply MUST NOT consume document IDs; existing\n  table handles and query caches MUST remain coherent after either.\n- **FR-012** (R05b): preview() MUST return a new, detached list on every\n  call; mutating a previously returned preview list MUST NOT affect later\n  preview results or any internal writer state.\n- **FR-013** (R05 clarification): apply() MUST advance the wrapper's\n  next-insertion-ID state on every successful batch and write that state back\n  to the underlying table so that subsequent inserts -- via apply() or direct\n  db.insert -- continue the ID sequence without reuse or rewind, even across\n  remove operations; a failed batch MUST NOT advance the sequence.\n- **FR-014** (R06): Direct db.insert operations performed between wrapper\n  calls MUST be seen by the wrapper; the wrapper MUST NOT cache a stale\n  initial snapshot of table data or ID state.\n\n### Key Entities\n\n- **TinyDB instance**: The supplied database that BatchWriter wraps; source of\n  the default table and of ordinary reads/writes. Not modified in its API.\n- **Default table**: The single table BatchWriter operates on; holds preexisting\n  and inserted documents; its complete data defines the pre-batch state for\n  rollback.\n- **Operation**: A dictionary describing one atomic step: an insert carrying a\n  document dict, or a remove carrying a positive integer document ID.\n- **Document**: A dict (possibly nested with lists/dicts) inserted into the\n  default table; stored as an independent copy of the caller's input.\n- **Document ID**: A positive integer identifying a document in the default\n  table; the unit of the return value of apply() and of remove targeting.\n\n## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*\n\n### Measurable Outcomes\n\n- **SC-001**: 100% of valid mixed insert/remove batches apply completely to the\n  default table and return exactly the inserted document IDs in insertion order\n  (removes contribute nothing).\n- **SC-002**: 100% of failing batches (missing-ID remove, malformed operation,\n  or error mid-batch) leave the default table byte-for-byte equal to its\n  pre-batch state, as observed through both the wrapper and previously acquired\n  table handles.\n- **SC-003**: 100% of unknown/malformed operations raise ValueError; empty\n  input returns [] in 100% of cases.\n- **SC-004**: 100% of ordinary TinyDB reads/writes and preexisting-document\n  accesses behave identically with and without the wrapper (no regressions in\n  the existing test suite).\n- **SC-005**: 0 aliasing faults: mutating caller input or returned data after\n  apply() never changes stored documents, for nested data at any depth.\n- **SC-006**: 100% of preview calls return exactly the IDs apply would\n  produce while leaving table contents and the next insertion ID unchanged,\n  including for batches that would fail.\n- **SC-007**: 0 ID-consumption faults: preview and failed apply never\n  consume or advance document IDs; every successful batch advances the\n  sequence exactly by the number of inserts it performed.\n- **SC-008**: 0 ID-reuse/rewind faults: after any mix of apply batches,\n  removes, and direct db.insert calls, the next inserted ID is strictly\n  greater than every ID used so far.\n- **SC-009**: 100% of structurally invalid inputs (bool doc_id, unsupported\n  fields, non-list operations, non-dict documents) raise ValueError with no\n  committed change; empty-dict documents are accepted in 100% of cases.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- **A1** (from R01): A 'positive integer' doc_id means an integer > 0; 0,\n  negatives, booleans, and non-integer values are malformed and raise\n  ValueError.\n- **A2** (from R02, resolved): \"Any error\" in atomicity includes malformed\n  operations (ValueError) and missing-ID removes; the error type for a\n  missing-ID remove is ValueError, as pinned by the public acceptance test.\n  Every such error MUST trigger rollback.\n- **A3** (from R02): \"Complete pre-batch default-table data\" is the exact set of\n  documents (with their IDs) present in the default table immediately before\n  apply() is called; rollback restores that exact state.\n- **A4** (from R03): \"Returned nested data\" refers to the returned list of\n  inserted document IDs plus the stored documents being independent copies of\n  the caller's input documents; apply() itself returns only IDs.\n- **A5** (scope): BatchWriter operates on the default table only; other tables\n  are out of scope and are not part of the batch/rollback contract.\n- **A6** (scope): The implementation lives in tinydb/journal.py; public\n  acceptance is acceptance_public.py; upstream tests remain unchanged; own\n  tests may be added.\n- **A7** (assumption): apply() is synchronous and single-threaded; concurrent\n  batch usage is out of scope for this milestone.\n- **A8** (conflict resolution): An older generated note claiming \"preview may\n  consume IDs and bool IDs are accepted\" is unverified and is superseded by\n  R04/R05; this specification follows R04/R05 (bool doc_ids rejected; preview\n  never consumes IDs). A previously passing happy-path smoke test does not\n  verify these boundaries and is not treated as evidence for them.\n- **A9** (from R05 clarification): \"Writing the next-insertion-ID state back\n  to the underlying table\" means the table's own next-ID bookkeeping (used by\n  ordinary inserts) reflects the highest ID consumed by any successful batch,\n  so direct db.insert continues the sequence; no separate persistent\n  counter file or new storage format is required.\n"}